course: everyday
lesson: 57
title: My body belongs to me
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your body belongs to you, and you can say no to any touch that you do not want or that feels wrong, even a hug or a tickle from someone you know; you can offer a high-five or a wave instead.
explain: Your private parts are the parts of your body covered by a swimsuit; nobody should touch them, ask you to touch theirs, or show you pictures of private parts, except a parent or carer helping keep you clean and healthy, or a doctor or nurse checking you with your parent or carer there.
explain: Surprises and secrets are different: a surprise, like a birthday present, is happy and is told soon, while a secret that makes you feel worried or uncomfortable is a secret you should never keep.
explain: If anything about a touch, a picture or a secret gives you an «uh-oh» feeling, say no if you can, get away, and tell a trusted adult; it is never the child's fault, and you will not be in trouble for telling.
explain: If the first adult you tell does not listen, keep telling other trusted adults until someone helps; good adults want to know, so that they can keep you safe.
explain: An adult told a boy in England that their touching games had to stay a secret; the boy felt confused and kept quiet for a long time, but when he finally told his teacher, it was stopped.
explain: A girl in Australia said no loudly when an older child touched her in a way she did not like, and told her mum straight away; the adults made sure it never happened again.
fact: Your body belongs to you.
fact: You can say no to touches you do not want, even from people you know and love.
fact: Private parts are the parts of the body covered by a swimsuit.
fact: Nobody should touch your private parts except to keep you clean and healthy.
fact: A doctor or nurse may check your body when your parent or carer is there.
fact: Nobody should ask you to touch their private parts or show you pictures of private parts.
fact: A surprise is happy and is told soon; a secret that makes you feel bad should be told.
fact: An adult who asks a child to keep a secret about touching is breaking a safety rule.
fact: If something feels wrong, you tell a trusted adult.
fact: It is never the child's fault if someone breaks a body safety rule.
fact: If one adult does not listen, you keep telling other trusted adults until someone helps.
fact: Other people's bodies belong to them too, so we ask before hugging and stop when someone says no.
fact: An adult who asks a child to keep a secret about touching is doing something wrong, and it is never the child's fault.
fact: Telling a trusted adult is how unsafe touching gets stopped.
exercise: Who does your body belong to? | To you | To your parents only | To any adult who looks after you | To your doctor
exercise: Your uncle wants a hug, but you do not feel like hugging today. What can you do? | Say no politely and offer a high-five or a wave | Hug him anyway, because he is family and family always comes first | Run away and hide in your room all day | Tell him he is not your uncle anymore
exercise: Which parts of the body are private parts? | The parts covered by a swimsuit | The hands and the feet | Any part that has a bruise | Only the parts that are ticklish
exercise: When is it all right for a doctor to check your body? | When your parent or carer is there with you | Whenever any adult says that they are a doctor or a nurse | Only when you are asleep | Never, even if you are ill
exercise: What is the difference between a surprise and a bad secret? | A surprise is happy and told soon; a bad secret makes you worried | A surprise is for adults and a secret is for children | There is no difference at all | A secret is always kept for ever, and a surprise is always told at once to everyone
exercise: Someone asks you to keep a secret about a touch that confused you. What do you do? | Tell a trusted adult, even though they said not to | Keep the secret, because you promised | Ask the person to explain it to you again, slowly, on your own | Forget about it and say nothing ever
exercise: What does an «uh-oh» feeling tell you? | That something might not be safe or right | That you are hungry and need a snack straight away | That you are about to win a game | That you should keep quiet
exercise: If someone breaks a body safety rule, whose fault is it? | Never the child's fault | The child's fault for not saying no | The child's fault if they were alone | Nobody's fault if it was a secret
exercise: You tell an adult that something felt wrong, but they do not listen. What next? | Tell another trusted adult until someone helps | Stop telling, because adults do not really care | Keep it secret so nobody gets upset | Wait until you are grown up and then tell somebody
exercise: Your little cousin says stop when you tickle her. What do you do? | Stop at once, because her body belongs to her | Keep going, because she is laughing, so she must like it | Stop only when you have finished | Tickle her softer instead
exercise: What is a safe surprise? | Keeping quiet about Grandma's birthday present until her party | Keeping quiet about a touch that confused you | Keeping quiet about a picture that upset you | Keeping quiet forever about something that worries you
exercise: Will you get in trouble for telling a trusted adult about an unsafe touch? | No, good adults want to know so they can help | Yes, because telling secrets is always wrong | Only if the person is someone you know well | Only if you waited a long time to tell
exercise: Someone online shows you a picture of private parts. What should you do? | Close it and tell a trusted adult | Keep looking so you can describe it later | Send it to a friend to ask about it | Say nothing in case you get in trouble
exercise: Why do we ask before hugging a friend? | Their body belongs to them, so they choose | Because hugging is against school rules | Because hugs spread illness every time, so they need warning | So that they owe us a hug later
test: Which is true? | You can say no to a kiss even from a relative | You must let any relative kiss you when they visit | Saying no to a hug is always rude | Only adults can say no to touches
test: A girl at the doctor's in Ottawa has her dad with her while the doctor checks a rash. Is this all right? | Yes, the doctor is checking her health with her dad there | No, doctors must never look at children | Only if she promises not to tell anyone about it afterwards | Only if the doctor is a woman
test: Which secret should a child tell a trusted adult? | A grown-up said «this is our secret, don't tell your mum» about a touch | The class is planning a surprise party for the teacher | Dad bought Mum flowers for tomorrow | Your sister is making you a birthday card
test: What would be the best thing to do if a touch gave you an «uh-oh» feeling? | Say no, get away and tell a trusted adult | Think that you must have imagined it and forget it | Wait to see if it happens again | Keep it to yourself so nobody worries
test: A boy says he did not say no to an unsafe touch because he was frozen with fear. Was it his fault? | No, it is never the child's fault | Yes, because he did not say no | Partly, because he was there | Yes, if he did not tell somebody straight away
test: Why do children keep telling until someone listens? | Every child deserves to be safe, and some adults miss it at first | Because adults like to hear things many times | Because telling once is against the rules | So that everyone in the school knows about it and can keep an eye out
test: Which is NOT a body safety rule? | You must always do whatever an adult says with your body | Your body belongs to you | You can say no to touches you do not want | Tell a trusted adult if something feels wrong
test: Ben wants to give his friend a hug after a goal, but his friend steps back. What should Ben do? | Give him a high-five instead | Hug him anyway because it was a great goal | Feel hurt and stop being his friend | Laugh at him for being shy
test: How long should a surprise be kept? | Only a short time, until the happy moment | Forever, like any other secret | Until an adult asks about it | As long as the person who told it to you wants it kept
test: uncle wants a hug n i dont want to. do i have to | No, you can say no even to people you love | Yes, family can always hug you | Yes, or you will hurt his feelings | Only say no to people you do not know or have never met
test: someone said "this is our secret dont tell ur mum" n it feels yuck | Tell a trusted adult, bad secrets should be told | Keep it, a promise is a promise | Keep it unless they say it again | Only tell if they ask you twice
test: doc wants to look at my rash down there?? | That is okay with your parent or carer there | Never okay, even with a parent there | Okay only if nobody else knows | Okay if the doctor says to keep it a secret from your parents
test: my friend said stop tickling me but its funny. keep going? | No, stop when someone says no | Yes, if you are both laughing | Yes, tickling is not real touching | Keep going until they really cry
test: someone said it's our special secret and i can't tell mum | Tell a trusted adult, secrets about touching are never okay | Keep it, you promised | Only tell if it happens again | Wait until you are older to tell, then they'll believe you
test: uncle wants a hug but i don't want to | You can say no and give a wave or high five | You must hug him, he is family and he'll be upset | Hug him so he is not sad | Hide in your room all day
test: Leo says if ur told not to tell it's ur fault if u do. right? | No, it is never your fault and telling is right | Yes, you broke a promise | Yes, you will get in trouble | Yes, unless it happens twice
test: my cousin says u have to hug ur aunties even if u don't want to cos it's rude not to | Not true; your body belongs to you, and you can say no politely | She is right, refusing is always rude | She is right, family can always touch you | She is right, unless you are ill or have a cold, then you can say no just that once
test: heard a grown-up told a kid to keep a secret about a game that made him feel weird, and he did | The real lesson: a secret that makes you feel bad must be told | It shows that secrets from grown-ups must always be kept, whatever they are about | It means games with adults are always fine | It is gossip, so secrets do not matter
test: someone says a girl in our year told the counsellor something private and now everyone's guessing what, should i join in | No; she did the right thing, and gossiping about it hurts her | Yes, because everyone is guessing | Yes, so the truth comes out | Yes, but only with your best friend, who promises never to tell anyone else
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the entitlement of a child in respect of unwanted touch? | A child may refuse touch he does not want, even from persons known and loved | A child may refuse touch only from strangers | A child may never refuse a relative | A child may refuse touch only upon producing a parent's written permission to do so
exam: Identify the circumstance in which a doctor or nurse may examine a child's body. | When the child's parent or carer is present | At any time without telling anyone | Only if the child keeps it secret | Only when the child is asleep
exam: It being given that an adult asks a child to keep a secret concerning touching, determine the proper characterisation of such conduct. | The adult is breaking a safety rule, and the child should tell a trusted adult | The adult is acting properly, and the secret should be kept, as a promise is binding | The child is at fault for being asked | The matter is of no importance
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance where something feels wrong. | To inform a trusted adult | To keep the feeling to oneself | To wait until it happens again | To tell only other children
